{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"2fdce4fb929b0a1c","name":"card_event_orders_from_above","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/NavalWarfare/events/card_event_orders_from_above","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"ORDERS FROM ABOVE","text":"Target a tank or infantry. Give it +1+1 for each damaged unit if friendly. Otherwise, deal 3 damage to it.","flavorText":"During WWII, the Soviet Union's highest military authority was the Stavka of the Supreme High Command, established on June 23, 1941, following the German invasion. Initially chaired by Marshal Semyon Timoshenko, leadership quickly transitioned to Joseph Stalin, who assumed the role of Supreme Commander-in-Chief from July 10, 1941. Key members included Georgy Zhukov, Vyacheslav Molotov, Kliment Voroshilov, Semyon Budyonny, and Admiral Nikolai Kuznetsov. The Stavka was responsible for strategic military planning and coordination for all Soviet Armed Forces. It oversaw the organization of campaigns, allocation of resources, and coordination between various military branches and partisan groups. The General Staff acted as its executive body, implementing the Stavka's directives. Throughout the war, the Stavka issued critical orders, such as Order No. 270, which mandated severe penalties for surrender, and Order No. 227, famously known as \"Not One Step Back,\" aimed at preventing unauthorized retreats. Operating primarily from Moscow, the Stavka adapted to wartime conditions by relocating to more secure locations, including underground facilities, in order to maintain command continuity during air raids. Following the end of hostilities in Europe, the Stavka was disbanded in October 1945, concluding its role in directing the Soviet war effort.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Rare","cost":2,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_orders_from_above","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_orders_from_above_title","SourceString":"ORDERS FROM ABOVE","LocalizedString":"ORDERS FROM ABOVE"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":2,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_orders_from_above_text","SourceString":"Target a tank or infantry. Give it +1+1 for each damaged unit if friendly. Otherwise, deal 3 damage to it.","LocalizedString":"Target a tank or infantry. Give it +1+1 for each damaged unit if friendly. Otherwise, deal 3 damage to it."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_orders_from_above_history","SourceString":"During WWII, the Soviet Union's highest military authority was the Stavka of the Supreme High Command, established on June 23, 1941, following the German invasion. Initially chaired by Marshal Semyon Timoshenko, leadership quickly transitioned to Joseph Stalin, who assumed the role of Supreme Commander-in-Chief from July 10, 1941. Key members included Georgy Zhukov, Vyacheslav Molotov, Kliment Voroshilov, Semyon Budyonny, and Admiral Nikolai Kuznetsov. The Stavka was responsible for strategic military planning and coordination for all Soviet Armed Forces. It oversaw the organization of campaigns, allocation of resources, and coordination between various military branches and partisan groups. The General Staff acted as its executive body, implementing the Stavka's directives. Throughout the war, the Stavka issued critical orders, such as Order No. 270, which mandated severe penalties for surrender, and Order No. 227, famously known as \"Not One Step Back,\" aimed at preventing unauthorized retreats. Operating primarily from Moscow, the Stavka adapted to wartime conditions by relocating to more secure locations, including underground facilities, in order to maintain command continuity during air raids. Following the end of hostilities in Europe, the Stavka was disbanded in October 1945, concluding its role in directing the Soviet war effort.","LocalizedString":"During WWII, the Soviet Union's highest military authority was the Stavka of the Supreme High Command, established on June 23, 1941, following the German invasion. Initially chaired by Marshal Semyon Timoshenko, leadership quickly transitioned to Joseph Stalin, who assumed the role of Supreme Commander-in-Chief from July 10, 1941. Key members included Georgy Zhukov, Vyacheslav Molotov, Kliment Voroshilov, Semyon Budyonny, and Admiral Nikolai Kuznetsov. The Stavka was responsible for strategic military planning and coordination for all Soviet Armed Forces. It oversaw the organization of campaigns, allocation of resources, and coordination between various military branches and partisan groups. The General Staff acted as its executive body, implementing the Stavka's directives. Throughout the war, the Stavka issued critical orders, such as Order No. 270, which mandated severe penalties for surrender, and Order No. 227, famously known as \"Not One Step Back,\" aimed at preventing unauthorized retreats. Operating primarily from Moscow, the Stavka adapted to wartime conditions by relocating to more secure locations, including underground facilities, in order to maintain command continuity during air raids. Following the end of hostilities in Europe, the Stavka was disbanded in October 1945, concluding its role in directing the Soviet war effort."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","selectTargetOnPlayedFromHand":true,"cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::NavalWarfare","soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Order_227'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Order_227.0"},"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/Soviet/NavalWarfare/t_orders_from_above.t_orders_from_above","SubPathString":""},"artist":"Soviet WWII Poster","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/ORDERS_FROM_ABOVE.ORDERS_FROM_ABOVE","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":344.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_orders_from_above_photo","SourceStri